   "format": "mcq",
   "stem": {
      "text": "The functions \\( f \\), \\( g \\), and \\( h \\) are continuous for all real numbers, with \\( f(0) = 4 \\), \\( f(1) = 0 \\), \\( f(2) = 2 \\), \\( f(3) = 2 \\), \\( f(4) = 1 \\), \\( g(0) = 2 \\), \\( g(1) = 1 \\), \\( g(2) = 3 \\), \\( g(3) = 4 \\), \\( g(4) = 2 \\), \\( h(0) = 4 \\), \\( h(1) = 1 \\), \\( h(2) = 0 \\), \\( h(3) = 4 \\), \\( h(4) = 4 \\). Find the value of \\( \\lim_{x \\to 2} \\frac{h(f(x)) + f(g(x))}{g(h(x))} \\)."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "symbolic",
      "mathjson": 1
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "Each function is continuous, so each limit at 2 is a function value: \\( f(2) = 2 \\), \\( g(2) = 3 \\), \\( h(2) = 0 \\).",
         "mathjson": 2
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "The expression is a quotient whose numerator is a sum of two composites and whose denominator is a composite. In each composite the outer function is continuous at the limit of the inner one, so \\( \\lim_{x \\to 2} h(f(x)) = h(2) = 0 \\), \\( \\lim_{x \\to 2} f(g(x)) = f(3) = 2 \\), \\( \\lim_{x \\to 2} g(h(x)) = g(0) = 2 \\).",
         "mathjson": 0
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "The denominator's limit is 2, which is not zero, so the quotient theorem applies.",
         "mathjson": 2
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "Apply the quotient theorem: \\( \\frac{0 + 2}{2} = 1 \\).",
         "mathjson": 1
      }
   ],
